        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "medium",
          "timestamp": "2026-07-21T13:34:47Z",
          "note": "writeModels() calls readModels() internally during the deletion guard phase (step 4). If local.json does not exist at all, the code catches all errors and treats existingModels as []. However, if local.json exists but has a schema validation error, the error is silently swallowed too (empty catch in writeModels). This means a corrupted local.json would allow a write to proceed as if it were empty, potentially bypassing the deletion guard. Consider narrowing the catch to ENOENT-only errors, similar to how readModels() handles it. Not a blocker for current AC, but a latent risk."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-07-21T13:34:47Z",
          "note": "No issues with the vi.mock scoping — the mock redirects WORKSPACE_ROOT to a per-test temp directory and is properly cleaned up in afterEach. No test-isolation leakage observed."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "code-review",
      "status": "FAIL",
      "started_at": "2026-07-21T13:35:09Z",
      "completed_at": "2026-07-21T13:37:18Z",
      "duration_ms": 129000,
      "summary": [
        "Code review FAIL — one blocking issue: writeModels() uses a bare `catch {}` (lines 202–207) that swallows ALL errors from readModels() during the deletion guard phase. A corrupted local.json (PARSE_ERROR or VALIDATION_ERROR from readModels) silently falls through to existingModels=[], making deletedModels empty and bypassing the deletion guard entirely. The module's documented guarantee — 'rejects deletion of models whose UUID is referenced in assignments.json' — is violated when local.json is corrupted. Fix: narrow the catch to ENOENT-only by re-throwing ApiError exceptions, analogous to the readModels() pattern at line 113–116. One fix-forward comment applied to loadDefaults() clarifying the dead-code path in its readModels catch block."
      ],
      "artifacts": {
        "files_modified": [
          "mcp-server/src/gui/model-registry.ts"
        ]
      },
      "comments": [
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "high",
          "timestamp": "2026-07-21T13:37:18Z",
          "note": "[BLOCKING] writeModels() deletion guard catch (lines 202–207): the bare `catch {}` swallows ALL errors thrown by readModels() — including ApiError PARSE_ERROR and VALIDATION_ERROR — treating them identically to ENOENT. With existingModels=[], deletedModels is always empty, so the deletion guard never fires. A caller that submits a new model list while local.json is corrupt will silently succeed even when it is deleting models referenced in assignments.json. FIX: replace the bare catch with a targeted re-throw for ApiError, keeping only the ENOENT-equivalent silent path. Pattern to follow (same file, readModels line 113): `if (isNodeError(err) && err.code === 'ENOENT') { ... } else { throw err; }`. For readModels(), the ENOENT case comes back as the ApiError wrapping is done after the catch — in writeModels(), readModels() is called directly, so throw any ApiError through: `try { existingModels = await readModels(); } catch (err) { if (!(err instanceof ApiError && err.code === 'READ_ERROR') && !(isNodeError(err) && err.code === 'ENOENT')) throw err; }`"
        },
        {
          "type": "reviewer-applied-fix",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-07-21T13:37:18Z",
          "note": "[Fix-Forward applied] loadDefaults() readModels catch block (line 346–353): the comment said 'local.json doesn\\'t exist — treat as empty' but this is misleading — the ENOENT case is handled inside readModels() via auto-init and never reaches this catch as READ_ERROR. The block is actually handling non-ENOENT OS errors (e.g. permission denied) on local.json. Also, VALIDATION_ERROR and PARSE_ERROR were not explicitly re-thrown here (the `else { throw err; }` branch handles it, but no comment clarified why). Updated the comment to accurately describe what each error code path means and why READ_ERROR is silently absorbed while other ApiErrors are re-thrown. No behavioral change."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-07-21T13:37:18Z",
          "note": "The _initializeLocalFromDefault() function uses a single-underscore prefix to signal 'internal only'. config.ts uses `isNodeError` without any prefix. Consider aligning to a single convention across the gui/ module (e.g. fully private via module closure, or a consistent `_` prefix). Since the function is unexported this is cosmetic, but worth standardizing for new contributors."
        },
        {
          "type": "documentation-forward",
          "priority": "medium",
          "timestamp": "2026-07-21T13:37:18Z",
          "note": "[documentation-forward] writeModels() JSDoc (lines 143–158) documents the deletion guard behavior but does not mention what happens when local.json does not yet exist (guard is skipped, write proceeds). Once the ENOENT narrowing fix is applied, also document what happens when local.json exists but is corrupt (write is now rejected with ApiError). Updating the `@throws` block and guard description in the JSDoc would make the function contract explicit for future API handler authors."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-07-21T13:37:18Z",
          "note": "loadDefaults() writes to disk only when toAdd.length > 0, but returns the merged array regardless. If the caller re-renders the UI from the returned models before the disk write completes (async), there is a brief TOCTOU window. A comment noting 'disk write is conditional on toAdd.length > 0; the returned models array always reflects the full merge' would clarify intent. (Developer noted this; confirming it warrants a comment rather than a structural change.)"
        }
      ]
    },
